<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fath-Ali Shah Qajar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1772–1834</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Fath Ali Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fath-Ali Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> was the second Qajar shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, reigning from 1797 to 1834 after his uncle Agha Mohammad Khan. His reign saw two wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, ended by the treaties of Gulistan in 1813 and Turkmenchay in 1828, which cost Iran its Caucasian lands. He is remembered for his elaborate court, long beard and many children.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar.jpg" alt="Fath-Ali Shah Qajar" width="320" height="648" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mihr 'Ali · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Fath_Ali_Shah_Standing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
